{"hits": {"hits": [{"created": "2026-10-15T12:50:31.888510+00:00", "modified": "2026-10-15T12:50:32.563685+00:00", "id": 23395703, "conceptrecid": "23395702", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23395703", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.23395702", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23395703", "metadata": {"title": "A seven-hyperplane counterexample to a generic-initial degree bound", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23395703", "publication_date": "2026-10-15", "description": "For the characteristic-zero arrangement Q=xy(x-y)(x-2y)zw(x+z+w), the reverse-lexicographic generic initial ideal of its ambient Jacobian ideal has pure-second-variable cutoff 8 and a degree 7 minimal generator whose largest variable is the third. A self-contained open-set proof refutes the general-dimensional degree bound of Bigatti\u2013Palezzato\u2013Torielli, also stated in OWR Report 5/2021. The valid three-variable arrangement theorem remains intact. The construction is a classical parallel connection; logarithmic, Hilbert\u2013Burch and generic-section methods are prior, and the applicable Migliore\u2013Nagel resolution of the distinct unmixed ideal is credited. A bounded audit through 15 October 2026 found no complete predecessor in its inspected corpus; historical priority and independent discovery remain unestablished. Ilardi (2018) full text was not obtained, and parameter families and historical code were not exhausted. The deposit includes the six-page note, LaTeX source and exact standard-library Python verification certificates and authored audits. AI tools were used extensively. This preprint is unrefereed and has not undergone conventional human peer review.", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Kriebel, Alec", "affiliation": "Independent researcher", "orcid": "0009-0001-9320-500X"}], "keywords": ["hyperplane arrangements", "generic initial ideals", "Jacobian ideals", "Hilbert\u2013Burch theorem", "Lefschetz properties", "sectional matrices", "counterexample"], "related_identifiers"